{{- /*
    One page per talk, built from the program of every event.

    The talks of this archive live in the front matter of the event that
    gave them, under the talks block, and that is where they stay: a talk
    is a record of one day, and the day owns it. This adapter reads those
    blocks and adds a page for each talk, so a talk gets a URL, a title, a
    photo, and its links, without 165 new files that would hold a second
    copy of data the event page already carries.

    What it adds per talk, and nothing more:

        path    <event file name>/<talk id>, so /talks/pyday_bcn_2025/...
        title   the title of the talk
        dates   the date of the event, as date and as lastmod
        params  talk, the id; event, the content path of the event page;
                title_lang, when the title is not in English

    Everything else is resolved when the page renders, by
    partials/talk_record.html, which reads the talk out of the event page
    through site.GetPage. So the description, the speakers, the language,
    the links, and the photo have one source, the event front matter, and
    cannot drift from what the program grid and the gallery of that day
    print.

    Why the event is part of the path. A talk id is unique inside its
    event and not across the archive: "lightning-talks-session-2-closing-
    session-kahoot" is both a row of PyDay BCN 2022 and a row of PyDay BCN
    2023, and a recurring session of a recurring event is exactly the kind
    of title that repeats. A flat /talks/<id>/ would need a tie-break, and
    every tie-break that keeps the bare id for one of the two depends on
    what else the site holds: importing an older event could then move the
    URL of a talk that is already published and already linked to. With the
    event in the path, the URL of a talk is a function of its own two ids
    and of nothing else, so no later import can move it. The event segment
    is the base name of its content file, the same segment its own URL
    uses, so the two read alike:

        /events/pyday_bcn/pyday_bcn_2025/
        /talks/pyday_bcn_2025/build-advanced-agentic-llm-apps-with-langgraph-and-langchain/

    Hugo builds no page for the middle segment, /talks/pyday_bcn_2025/: a
    content adapter adds the pages it is asked for and creates no section
    node above them. The index at /talks/ lists every talk of every year,
    so nothing is only reachable through a URL that is not built.

    Why this template reads the files itself. A content adapter runs while
    the content is still being assembled, so site.Pages is not available
    yet: Hugo answers "this method cannot be called before the site is
    fully initialized". The event front matter is therefore read from disk
    with os.ReadFile and parsed with transform.Unmarshal. Every file under
    content/events/ opens with a YAML front matter block and holds exactly
    one line that is "---" on its own, which is the one that closes it, so
    splitting on the first "\n---" is safe. A file with no front matter, or
    with no talks block, adds nothing.

    Why the front matter goes through resources.FromString first. Given a
    plain string, transform.Unmarshal has to guess the format, and it
    guesses by which of "," "{" "=" ":" "<" appears first. So a front
    matter that opens with a comment holding a comma, before the colon of
    its first field, is read as CSV, and the whole build stops with "record
    on line 3: wrong number of fields" and no file name in it. That is what
    the 266 meetup files did when they arrived: each one opens with the
    comment that says which export record it came from. Wrapping the text
    in a resource named .yaml states the format instead of leaving it to
    the order of the punctuation, so a comment is just a comment again.
    The name is the base name of the event file, which makes a parse error
    name the file it came from.

    The walk covers content/events/ and one level of directories under it,
    which is the shape of that section: the four series, pyday_bcn,
    pydatabcn, other_events and meetups, each with its editions. A
    directory nested deeper logs a warning rather than losing its talks in
    silence.

    One more page per type of talk. /talks/ lists every row and offers a
    tab per type above the table (see partials/talks_tabs.html), and each
    tab is a page of its own that lists only the rows of that type:

        /talks/type/workshop/
        /talks/type/lightning/

    The filter is sub-pages and not JavaScript, so a reader with no script, a crawler, and a link in a message all
    reach the same list. A page exists only for a type that at least one
    talk of the archive carries: the vocabulary of partials/event_types.html
    holds eleven types, and a coffee break is one of them, but no talk is
    a coffee break, so /talks/type/coffee/ is not built and no tab names
    it. The type of a talk is read here the way partials/listing_counts.html
    and partials/event_type_icon.html read it, lower case and trimmed, so
    the pages this adapter adds are the types those partials count. The
    path segment is the token of the vocabulary and not the noun, because
    the token is what the front matter writes and the noun may be
    reworded; one directory for all of them, type/, so the segment can
    never collide with the base name of an event file. The page carries
    the token as params.filter, which is how every walker of the talks
    section tells a listing from a talk (they skip a page with a filter),
    and how layouts/talks/talk_type.html knows which rows to keep.
*/ -}}
{{- $root := "content/events" -}}

{{- /* The files to read: the markdown of content/events/ and of each series
       under it. An _index.md is the container of a section and carries no
       program of its own. */ -}}
{{- $files := slice -}}
{{- range $entry := os.ReadDir $root -}}
    {{- $here := path.Join $root $entry.Name -}}
    {{- if $entry.IsDir -}}
        {{- range $child := os.ReadDir $here -}}
            {{- if $child.IsDir -}}
                {{- warnf "talks adapter: %q is nested deeper than this walk reads, so any talk in it gets no page." (path.Join $here $child.Name) -}}
            {{- else -}}
                {{- $files = $files | append (path.Join $here $child.Name) -}}
            {{- end -}}
        {{- end -}}
    {{- else -}}
        {{- $files = $files | append $here -}}
    {{- end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /* The types the talks of the archive carry, as a set, filled by the
       loop below and read after it for the pages per type. */ -}}
{{- $types := dict -}}

{{- range $file := $files -}}
    {{- if ne (path.Ext $file) ".md" }}{{ continue }}{{ end -}}
    {{- if eq (path.Base $file) "_index.md" }}{{ continue }}{{ end -}}
    {{- $raw := os.ReadFile $file -}}
    {{- if not (hasPrefix $raw "---") }}{{ continue }}{{ end -}}
    {{- $front := strings.TrimPrefix "---" (index (split $raw "\n---") 0) -}}
    {{- $event := resources.FromString (printf "events/%s.yaml" (path.BaseName $file)) $front | transform.Unmarshal -}}
    {{- with $event.talks -}}
        {{- /* The content path of the event page, which site.GetPage reads
               at render time, and the segment its URL uses. */ -}}
        {{- $path := strings.TrimSuffix ".md" (strings.TrimPrefix "content" $file) -}}
        {{- $slug := path.BaseName $file -}}
        {{- /* The date of the event is the date of every talk of it. One
               edition of the section carries no date, Hacktoberfest 2018,
               and it carries no talks either; a program with no date
               would still get its pages, dated by the build. */ -}}
        {{- $dates := dict -}}
        {{- with $event.date -}}
            {{- $dates = dict "date" (time.AsTime .) "lastmod" (time.AsTime .) -}}
        {{- end -}}
        {{- range $talk := . -}}
            {{- if not $talk.id -}}
                {{- warnf "talks adapter: a talk of %q has no id, so it gets no page." $file -}}
                {{- continue -}}
            {{- end -}}
            {{- with $talk.type -}}
                {{- $types = merge $types (dict (lower (trim (printf "%v" .) " ")) true) -}}
            {{- end -}}
            {{- $params := dict "talk" $talk.id "event" $path -}}
            {{- with $talk.title_lang -}}
                {{- $params = merge $params (dict "title_lang" .) -}}
            {{- end -}}
            {{- $.AddPage (dict
                    "kind" "page"
                    "path" (printf "%s/%s" $slug $talk.id)
                    "title" $talk.title
                    "dates" $dates
                    "params" $params) -}}
        {{- end -}}
    {{- end -}}
{{- end -}}

{{- /* The page of each type that has a talk. The title is the plural noun
       of the vocabulary with a capital, "Workshops", the words the tab
       reads; a token the vocabulary does not hold gets no page, and a
       warning, because no row would draw a marker for it either. The
       tokens are sorted so that two builds add the pages in one order. */ -}}
{{- $vocabulary := partialCached "event_types.html" "event_types" -}}
{{- range $token, $_ := $types -}}
    {{- $entry := index $vocabulary $token -}}
    {{- if not $entry -}}
        {{- warnf "talks adapter: type %q is not in partials/event_types.html, so it gets no page of its own." $token -}}
        {{- continue -}}
    {{- end -}}
    {{- $.AddPage (dict
            "kind" "page"
            "path" (printf "type/%s" $token)
            "title" (humanize $entry.many)
            "layout" "talk_type"
            "params" (dict
                "filter" $token
                "description" (printf "Every %s of every PyBCN event, with its speakers, its photo, its video, and its links." $entry.one))) -}}
{{- end -}}
